  "abstract_html": "<p>For a torus knot <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> the traceless <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">U</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> character variety is finite and nondegenerate, so Daemi and Scaduto’s S-complex for singular instanton homology can be built on it. The resulting complex has rank <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C3;</mi></mrow></math> is the signature, and by theorems of Li and Ye and of Kronheimer and Mrowka its homology <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>&#x0266E;</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> has rank <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><msub><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><msub><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math>, the sum of the absolute values of the coefficients of the Alexander polynomial; so the differential has rank <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mi>&#x00394;</mi><msub><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. We express <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> through floor sums and show that, for coprime <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>&#x0230A;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>b</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>4</mn><mi>&#x0230B;</mi></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> is the inverse of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>q</mi></mrow></math> modulo <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow></math>, and that the differential vanishes if and only if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>, or <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow></math> is odd and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>&#x02261;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> or <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>&#x02261;</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mo>&#x00028;</mo><mi>mod</mi><mspace width=\"0.333em\" /><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>; the ‘if’ direction is due to Daemi and Scaduto. By a result of Daemi and Scaduto, the component of the differential to the reducible generator is nonzero over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x0211A;</mi></mrow></math> exactly when their invariant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>h</mi></mrow></math> is positive. We show that the components from degree <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></math> to degree <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> and from degree <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> to degree <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> have ranks <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x0230A;</mi><mi>R</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x0230B;</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x02308;</mi><mi>R</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x02309;</mi></mrow></math>, independently of the choices in the construction. In particular the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x02124;</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></math>-graded group <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>&#x0266E;</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>;</mi><mi>&#x0211A;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> depends only on <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C3;</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mi>&#x00394;</mi><msub><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math>, and we give it explicitly. In each small-perturbation pillowcase computation of Hedden, Herald and Kirk, a bigon ends at the reducible generator exactly when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. We formulate a chain-level conjecture identifying the two complexes over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">F</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></math>; it predicts this pattern whenever the map to the reducible is nonzero modulo <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>.</p>",
